@page "/docs/quickstart"
@layout DocsLayout
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Quickstart — Vaultex</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-eyebrow">Get started</div>
<h1>Quickstart</h1>
<p class="docs-lead">
    Sign your first user in through Vaultex in five minutes. By the end of this guide you'll have a
    working OAuth 2.0 + PKCE flow against a Vaultex sandbox tenant.
</p>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--info">
    <span class="docs-callout-icon">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M12 2C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2zm-2 15l-5-5 1.41-1.41L10 14.17l7.59-7.59L19 8l-9 9z"/></svg>
    </span>
    <div class="docs-callout-body">
        <div class="docs-callout-title">Before you start</div>
        <p>You'll need a Vaultex tenant and an OIDC client registered for your app. If you don't have one, register at <a href="/account/register">/account/register</a> and create a client from the admin portal.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="step-1">1. Register a client</h2>
<p>
    Sign in to the Vaultex admin portal and open <strong>Clients → New client</strong>. Pick the
    application type that matches what you're building:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Web (server-side)</strong> &mdash; ASP.NET Core, Express, Django, Rails, etc.</li>
    <li><strong>SPA</strong> &mdash; React, Vue, Angular running in the browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Native / Mobile</strong> &mdash; iOS, Android, desktop apps.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Set the <strong>redirect URI</strong> to where Vaultex should send users after they sign in &mdash;
    typically <code>https://yourapp.com/signin-oidc</code> for ASP.NET Core or
    <code>https://yourapp.com/callback</code> for SPAs.
</p>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warn">
    <span class="docs-callout-icon">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M1 21h22L12 2 1 21zm12-3h-2v-2h2v2zm0-4h-2v-4h2v4z"/></svg>
    </span>
    <div class="docs-callout-body">
        <div class="docs-callout-title">Save your client secret</div>
        <p>Confidential clients (web apps with a backend) get a client secret. It's only shown once. Public clients (SPAs, mobile) don't get one &mdash; they use PKCE for security.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="step-2">2. Install the SDK</h2>
<p>Use whichever client library matches your stack. All of them speak standard OpenID Connect.</p>

<div class="docs-tabs">
    <div class="docs-tabs-bar">
        <button type="button" class="docs-tab @(installTab=="net"?"active":"")" @onclick='() => installTab = "net"'>.NET</button>
        <button type="button" class="docs-tab @(installTab=="node"?"active":"")" @onclick='() => installTab = "node"'>Node.js</button>
        <button type="button" class="docs-tab @(installTab=="react"?"active":"")" @onclick='() => installTab = "react"'>React</button>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-tab-panel @(installTab=="net"?"active":"")">
<pre><code>dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OpenIdConnect</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-tab-panel @(installTab=="node"?"active":"")">
<pre><code>npm install openid-client express-session</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-tab-panel @(installTab=="react"?"active":"")">
<pre><code>npm install oidc-client-ts react-oidc-context</code></pre>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="step-3">3. Configure the client</h2>
<p>
    Point your application at the Vaultex authority URL and pass the client credentials you got in
    step 1.
</p>

<div class="docs-tabs">
    <div class="docs-tabs-bar">
        <button type="button" class="docs-tab @(configTab=="net"?"active":"")" @onclick='() => configTab = "net"'>.NET</button>
        <button type="button" class="docs-tab @(configTab=="node"?"active":"")" @onclick='() => configTab = "node"'>Node.js</button>
        <button type="button" class="docs-tab @(configTab=="react"?"active":"")" @onclick='() => configTab = "react"'>React</button>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-tab-panel @(configTab=="net"?"active":"")">
<pre><code>// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(o =&gt;
{
    o.DefaultScheme         = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    o.DefaultChallengeScheme = OpenIdConnectDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
})
.AddCookie()
.AddOpenIdConnect(o =&gt;
{
    o.Authority    = "https://auth.vaultex.io";
    o.ClientId     = "your-client-id";
    o.ClientSecret = "your-client-secret";
    o.ResponseType = "code";
    o.UsePkce      = true;
    o.Scope.Add("openid");
    o.Scope.Add("profile");
    o.Scope.Add("email");
});

app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-tab-panel @(configTab=="node"?"active":"")">
<pre><code>import { Issuer, generators } from "openid-client";

const issuer = await Issuer.discover("https://auth.vaultex.io");
const client = new issuer.Client({
  client_id:     "your-client-id",
  client_secret: "your-client-secret",
  redirect_uris: ["https://yourapp.com/callback"],
  response_types: ["code"],
});</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-tab-panel @(configTab=="react"?"active":"")">
<pre><code>import { AuthProvider } from "react-oidc-context";

const oidcConfig = {
  authority:    "https://auth.vaultex.io",
  client_id:    "your-spa-client-id",
  redirect_uri: "https://yourapp.com/callback",
  scope:        "openid profile email",
};

&lt;AuthProvider {...oidcConfig}&gt;
  &lt;App /&gt;
&lt;/AuthProvider&gt;</code></pre>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="step-4">4. Trigger the sign-in</h2>
<p>
    Send the user to your login route. The framework redirects them to Vaultex, the user signs in,
    and Vaultex sends them back to your redirect URI with an authorization code.
</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">C#</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>[Authorize]
public class HomeController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Index() =&gt; View(User.Identity);
}</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
    The first time a user hits an authorized endpoint, ASP.NET Core challenges them. They're
    redirected to Vaultex, sign in (or register), then bounce back to your app with a session cookie
    set.
</p>

<h2 id="step-5">5. Read the user's identity</h2>
<p>
    After sign-in, the user's claims are available on <code>HttpContext.User</code>. Pull the
    fields you need:
</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">C#</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>var sub   = User.FindFirst("sub")?.Value;
var name  = User.FindFirst("name")?.Value;
var email = User.FindFirst("email")?.Value;
var roles = User.FindAll("role").Select(c =&gt; c.Value);</code></pre>
</div>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--success">
    <span class="docs-callout-icon">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M9 16.17L4.83 12l-1.42 1.41L9 19 21 7l-1.41-1.41L9 16.17z"/></svg>
    </span>
    <div class="docs-callout-body">
        <div class="docs-callout-title">That's it</div>
        <p>Your app now signs users in through Vaultex. From here, take a look at <a href="/docs/concepts/tokens">how tokens work</a> or jump to the <a href="/docs/guides/dotnet">full .NET integration guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

